This formula originates from the properties of matrix inversion and the definition of the metric tensor. Specifically, it is derived using the calculus of variations applied to the relationship $g^{\mu\alpha}g_{\alpha
u} = \delta^{\mu}_{
u}$. Taking the variation of both sides, we get $\delta(g^{\mu\alpha}g_{\alpha
u}) = \delta(\delta^{\mu}_{
u})$. Since the Kronecker delta $\delta^{\mu}_{
u}$ is a constant, its variation is zero. Expanding the left side using the product rule for variations, we have $(\delta g^{\mu\alpha})g_{\alpha
u} + g^{\mu\alpha}(\delta g_{\alpha
u}) = 0$. Rearranging and raising the index $
u$ using the metric tensor $g^{
u eta}$ and lowering the index $\alpha$ using the metric tensor $g_{\alpha eta}$ (implicitly in the $\delta g_{\alpha eta}$ term), we arrive at the stated formula.
